\chapter{The Play Viewer}
\label{viewer}

The interactive viewer uses [[curses]] to show one commit at a
time with vi-style navigation.
We chose [[curses]] over a prompt-based loop because single-keypress
navigation feels natural for browsing a log---the user never needs
to press Enter.
[[curses]] is a standard library module on Unix systems, which
covers university lab machines and most students' Macs; on Windows
the standard library lacks it, so the package depends on
[[windows-curses]] there (a [[sys_platform]] marker in
[[pyproject.toml]]), which provides the same module on top of
PDCurses.  The viewer therefore uses only the portable subset:
[[curses.wrapper]], the arrow-key constants, and the eight named
colours on a default background that [[curses_styles]] allocates
(\cref{sec:curses-styles}).

What the viewer draws it does not decide.  Both the reading view and
the raw view are lists of [[StyledLine]]s built by [[render]]
(\cref{render}), and this chapter is only the part that puts them on a
terminal: it turns each line's kind into a curses attribute, clips the
line to the window, and moves between records.
Batch mode (\cref{sec:play-batch}) takes the same lists through
[[ansi_lines]] instead.
That is what keeps the two media from drifting apart---a wording or a
colour changes in one place and both surfaces follow---and it is why
the viewer needs no knowledge of diffs, run blocks or I/O logs at all.

The viewer state is simple: the current commit index and a vertical
scroll offset within the commit's content.
We cache the detail for the currently displayed commit to avoid
re-fetching on every scroll.


