\section{Imports}

We import [[sys]] for [[play_batch]]'s stdout access and
[[get_commit_detail]] from the commits module for fetching
the display data.
[[get_run_trailers]] comes along because a commit only becomes a
complete run record once its trailer is merged back in
(\cref{sec:run-join}).
The [[curses]] import is deferred to inside methods because
it is unavailable on Windows---we only need it when the user
actually invokes the interactive viewer.

The package itself comes in as a plain module import rather than as
\mintinline{python}{from learnlog import record_view}, so that the
name is resolved when the viewer calls it rather than when the viewer
is loaded.
Today both spellings work---the package imports [[capture]],
[[gitrepo]] and [[_autostart]] and nothing else, so [[viewer]] is
always reached from the outside through [[cli]], long after
[[learnlog/__init__.py]] has finished.
The module import is the spelling that survives that ceasing to be
true: a [[from]] import inside an import cycle fails at load time with
an [[ImportError]] naming the wrong culprit, and it would fail in the
one module whose whole job is to be invisible to the run it reports
on.
The cost is an attribute lookup per navigation keystroke.

From [[render]] we take the two line builders, the two ways of
turning a kind into an attribute, and the three kind names this
chapter mentions by hand: [[chrome]] for the header and footer bars,
[[faded]] for an I/O prefix, and [[dim]] for the placeholder that
stands in for a filtered-away commit.
Every other kind passes through untouched, which is the point---the
viewer looks its attributes up by name and never enumerates them.

