\section{The [[PlaybackViewer]] class}

[[raw]] and [[color]] arrive as constructor arguments rather than as
arguments to [[_draw]] because they are properties of the session the
student asked for, not of a redraw: every keystroke would otherwise
have to carry them down three call levels to reach the line builder.

[[styles]] and [[page]] start at values that make sense with no
terminal behind them.
Curses fills both in---[[styles]] when the loop starts, [[page]] when
a draw measures the window---but [[_draw]] and [[_handle_key]] have to
be callable before either has happened, and that is not only a test
convenience: an empty [[styles]] is exactly the state a monochrome
terminal ends up in, and \mintinline{python}{styles.get(kind, 0)} then
draws every line with no attribute rather than raising.

<<viewer classes>>=
class PlaybackViewer:
    """Interactive curses-based commit viewer.

    Displays commits one at a time with vi-style navigation:
    ``H``/``L`` for previous/next commit, ``j``/``k`` for
    scrolling within a commit, ``PgUp``/``PgDn`` (or ``b``
    and space) for paging it, ``q`` to quit.  With ``raw``
    true the records are shown in the stored log format
    instead of the reading view.  ``color`` is ``True`` or
    ``False`` to force colour on or off, ``None`` to use it
    whenever the terminal has it.
    """

    def __init__(self, repo, commits, start_index=0,
                 filter_internal=True, trailers=None,
                 tag_map=None, raw=False, color=None):
        self.repo = repo
        self.commits = commits
        self.index = start_index
        self.scroll = 0
        self.filter_internal = filter_internal
        self.trailers = trailers
        self.tag_map = tag_map
        self.raw = raw
        self.color = color
        self.styles = {}
        self.page = DEFAULT_PAGE
        self._detail_cache = {}

    <<player methods>>
@

The trailer map (\cref{sec:run-join}) is the same data for every
commit, so we read it once and keep it.
We read it on first use rather than in the constructor: the viewer
redraws on every keystroke, so re-reading per redraw would make
navigation crawl on a large repository, while doing it in the
constructor would make merely \emph{creating} a viewer touch the
repository.

<<player methods>>=
def _get_trailers(self):
    """Return the trailer map, reading it on first use."""
    if self.trailers is None:
        self.trailers = get_run_trailers(self.repo)
    return self.trailers
@

The tag map is read the same way and, since the header and the card
both want it, through one accessor.
Two lazy readers of the same map would each be correct and together
would read it twice, and the second read is the one that happens on a
keystroke.

<<player methods>>=
def _tags_for(self, commit):
    """The tag names on ``commit``, reading the map once."""
    if self.tag_map is None:
        self.tag_map = get_tag_map(self.repo)
    return self.tag_map.get(commit["hash"], [])
@


\subsection{What counts as looking at a commit}
\label{sec:viewer-view-trace}

Playback is itself a recorded run (\cref{sec:review-runs}), and what
makes that record worth keeping is the trace of which commits the
student actually looked at.
The viewer contributes to it by reporting each commit it puts on
screen to [[learnlog.record_view]].

The unit of the trace is the commit that ends up \emph{drawn}, not the
key that produced it, and the two part company often.
[[j]] and [[k]] scroll within the commit already showing; [[L]] at the
last commit and [[g]] at the first one move nothing at all.
A trace built from keystrokes would report six visits to a commit the
student scrolled through once, and a reader cannot tell that apart
from six genuine returns---so the derived measure everyone will want,
\enquote{which run did they keep coming back to}, would be wrong in
exactly the cases it is asked about.
Hence the rule: a movement that leaves the position where it was
records nothing.

Two events are outside the rule for the same reason, that the position
is not what changed.
[[open]] is recorded unconditionally, because the first commit is on
screen through the viewer starting rather than through anything
moving; and a return to a commit seen before \emph{is} recorded again,
because the back-and-forth is the signal, not noise to be
deduplicated.

<<player methods>>=
def _record_view(self, action):
    """Report the commit now on screen to the run recorder."""
    learnlog.record_view(
        self.commits[self.index]["hash"], action,
    )
@

[[record_view]] does nothing when no run is being recorded, so the
viewer never has to ask whether it is being logged---the same bargain
the stream wrappers make with a student's program
(\cref{sec:review-runs}).
That is what lets [[learnlog play -f bundle.learnlog]] from an unwired
interpreter, and a unit test driving [[_handle_key]] directly, share
one unconditional call site with the recorded case.

All movement between commits goes through one method, so that the
\enquote{only when it changed} rule is stated once instead of in each
of the four key branches that could get it wrong independently.

<<player methods>>=
def _go_to(self, index, action):
    """Show commit ``index``, recording it if it changed."""
    moved = index != self.index
    self.index = index
    self.scroll = 0
    if moved:
        self._record_view(action)
@

Resetting [[scroll]] is outside the [[if]] deliberately: pressing
[[G]] while already at the last commit still scrolls back to the top
of it, which is what it always did and what the key means.
Only the trace treats that keypress as a non-event.


\subsection{Running the viewer}

The [[run]] method enters curses mode and drives the main loop.
We use [[curses.wrapper]] to ensure the terminal is restored
even if an exception occurs.

<<player methods>>=
def run(self):
    """Enter curses mode and run the interactive viewer."""
    import curses
    curses.wrapper(self._main_loop)
@


\subsection{The main loop}

Each iteration draws the screen and waits for a keypress.
We redraw on every key because the state may have changed.

Colour is set up once, here, because allocating colour pairs is a
property of the terminal session and not of a redraw.
[[curses_styles]] (\cref{sec:curses-styles}) does the allocating and
owns the guarding: it inherits the terminal's own foreground and
background through [[curses.use_default_colors]] so that light themes,
transparency and custom schemes survive, and it falls back to bold and
reverse where colour is unavailable.
The call it replaces was a bare [[curses.use_default_colors()]] here,
which is a latent crash: on a terminal without colour support that
call raises [[curses.error]], and it was the first thing the loop did,
so the viewer would have died before drawing anything.
[[--no-color]] skips the setup entirely rather than asking for a
monochrome table, which leaves [[styles]] empty and every
\mintinline{python}{styles.get(kind, 0)} answering \enquote{no
attribute}---the same state a monochrome terminal produces, reached by
one path instead of two.

Hiding the cursor is guarded for the same reason, and it is the same
terminals: [[curs_set]] fails wherever [[use_default_colors]] does
(a [[vt100]] or a [[dumb]] entry has neither capability), so moving
the crash off the colour setup and leaving it on the next line would
have fixed nothing a student could notice.
A visible cursor parked in a corner is a blemish; refusing to open the
viewer is not being able to read your own log.

The [[open]] event is recorded here rather than in [[_draw]], because
[[_draw]] runs again on every scroll keystroke and would turn one
opening into a stream of them.
Recording it before the first draw rather than after also keeps the
trace truthful about a viewer the student quits immediately: the
commit was on screen, so it belongs in the record.

<<player methods>>=
def _main_loop(self, stdscr):
    """Main event loop inside curses."""
    import curses
    if self.color is not False:
        self.styles = curses_styles(curses)
    try:
        curses.curs_set(0)
    except curses.error:
        pass
    stdscr.clear()
    self._record_view("open")

    while True:
        self._draw(stdscr)
        key = stdscr.getch()
        if not self._handle_key(key):
            break
@


\subsection{Drawing the screen}

The screen has three regions: a header showing position and
commit subject, the scrollable content area with the record itself,
and a footer with key bindings.

We build the content as a list of styled lines, then display the
portion that fits in the terminal window starting from the
scroll offset.
This approach avoids the complexity of [[curses]] pads while
handling arbitrarily long diffs.

We cache the [[git show]] output because fetching it shells out to
Git.
Without caching, every scroll keystroke would re-run the command,
making the viewer sluggish on large repositories.

We ask for git's own order rather than the reordered text.
Both views want it: the card lays the parts out itself from
[[split_commit_detail]] (\cref{sec:record-lines}), and raw mode is
supposed to show what the log stores, which is the order git prints.
Reordering here would mean undoing it in one view and printing a
rearrangement in the other.

<<player methods>>=
def _get_detail(self, commit_hash):
    """Fetch and cache the ``git show`` output."""
    if commit_hash not in self._detail_cache:
        self._detail_cache[commit_hash] = \
            get_commit_detail(
                self.repo, commit_hash,
                self.filter_internal,
                self._get_trailers(),
                reorder=False,
            )
    return self._detail_cache[commit_hash]
@

[[get_commit_detail]] answers [[None]] for a commit whose every run
block was filtered away (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}), but the viewer
still has to draw something: the commit list it pages through was built
before filtering, and silently drawing the neighbour instead would make
the position counter disagree with the [[H]]/[[L]] keys.
So we draw a placeholder in its place.
Since review runs are shown like any other run, that placeholder is
reserved for genuine plumbing---the [[pip]] install from
[[learnlog init]], and records of other subcommands left by older
versions.

[[curses]] is imported once at the top of the method rather than
inside the header and footer chunks.
Before, [[<<draw content area>>]] caught [[curses.error]] while
relying on [[<<draw header>>]] having bound the name a few lines
earlier---correct only for as long as nobody reorders the regions or
reuses the content chunk elsewhere, and it would fail as a
[[NameError]] raised from an [[except]] clause, which is about the
least legible way for a display bug to present itself.

<<player methods>>=
def _draw(self, stdscr):
    """Render the current commit to the screen."""
    import curses
    chrome = self.styles.get(KIND_CHROME, 0)

    stdscr.erase()
    height, width = stdscr.getmaxyx()

    commit = self.commits[self.index]
    detail = self._get_detail(commit["hash"])

    <<draw header>>
    <<build content lines>>
    <<draw content area>>
    <<draw footer>>

    stdscr.refresh()
@

The header says where the reader is and what they are looking at, and
which of those two it can say depends on the view.
The reading view names the record the way the card does, through
[[record_title]] (\cref{sec:record-title}), so the fixed bar and the
scrolling card agree on what the run was called.
Raw mode keeps the older bracketed form with the short hash, because
there the hash \emph{is} the thing on screen: the reader who asked for
the stored format is the reader who wants to hand that hash to
[[git]].
Tags are shown in both, spelled the same way in both, and the spelling
matches the batch card's [[Tags:]] line to the name---a tag is a
boundary every report reads (\cref{sec:metrics-tags}), so the two
places a student looks at their history must show where the milestones
sit.

The text is a method of its own so a test can read it without a
terminal.
We truncate to the terminal width to avoid curses errors.

<<player methods>>=
def _header_text(self, commit):
    """The header line for `commit`, untruncated."""
    names = self._tags_for(commit)
    tags = f" (tag: {', '.join(names)})" if names else ""
    if self.raw:
        return (
            f"[{self.index + 1}/{len(self.commits)}] "
            f"{commit['hash'][:SHORT_HASH_LEN]}{tags} "
            f"\u2014 {commit['subject']}"
        )
    return (
        f"{self.index + 1} of {len(self.commits)} \u2014 "
        f"{record_title(commit['subject'])}{tags}"
    )
@

The bars are drawn with the [[chrome]] kind rather than a literal
[[curses.A_REVERSE]].
The attribute is the same one, but naming it means the header, the
footer and the batch surface all read the decision out of
[[render]]'s style table instead of each restating it.
The lookup sits at the top of [[_draw]], beside the [[curses]]
import, for the reason the import does: both bars use it, and a name
bound inside one region's chunk and read in another's is the
dependency this method just stopped having.

