A styled line is at most two segments---a faded prefix and the text
after it (\cref{sec:styled-line})---and drawing it means turning that
into positions and attributes.
Doing the arithmetic in a function keeps the draw loop to what it is
about, and gives the clipping rule somewhere to be tested without a
terminal.

The rule is that the prefix and the text together stop before the last
column.
Curses treats a write into the bottom-right cell as an error, and the
prefix has already consumed part of the row by the time the text is
placed, so slicing the text to [[width - 1]] on its own would still
overflow by exactly the length of the prefix.
Wide characters still count as one column here, so a line of CJK text
or emoji overflows as it always has; fixing that needs a width-aware
measure such as [[wcwidth]] and is out of scope.

<<viewer functions>>=
def _segments(styled, width):
    """Split a styled line into ``(column, text, kind)``.

    The prefix is drawn faded from column 0 and the text
    follows it, both clipped so the pair ends before the
    window's last column.  Returns an empty list when there is
    no room at all.
    """
    room = width - 1
    if room <= 0:
        return []
    prefix = styled.prefix[:room]
    text = styled.text[:room - len(prefix)]
    segments = []
    if prefix:
        segments.append((0, prefix, KIND_FADED))
    if text:
        segments.append((len(prefix), text, styled.kind))
    return segments
@

We display lines starting from the scroll offset, filling
the area between the header (row~0) and the footer
(last row).
The page step is set here because this is where the window's height is
known; leaving two lines of overlap means the reader keeps a little
context across a page rather than jumping over the seam.

The [[try]] wraps a whole row, not each segment: a row that curses
refuses is lost either way, and the loop must go on to the rows below
it, which is the failure this guard has always been about.

<<draw content area>>=
content_start = CONTENT_START_ROW
content_end = height - FOOTER_OFFSET_FROM_BOTTOM
visible = content_end - content_start
self.page = max(1, visible - 2)

self.scroll = max(
    0,
    min(self.scroll,
        len(content_lines) - visible),
)

for i in range(visible):
    line_idx = self.scroll + i
    if line_idx >= len(content_lines):
        break
    try:
        for column, text, kind in _segments(
            content_lines[line_idx], width,
        ):
            stdscr.addstr(
                content_start + i, column, text,
                self.styles.get(kind, 0),
            )
    except curses.error:
        pass
@

