\subsection{Verifying the drawing}

Drawing was the one part of the viewer no test could reach, because
[[curses]] insists on a real terminal.
What it does not insist on is that [[stdscr]] be one of \emph{its}
windows: [[_draw]] calls four methods on the object it is handed, so a
stand-in that answers those four and writes down what it was asked to
draw turns every question about the screen into a question about a
list.
That is worth having beyond the coverage---the assertions below are
about which attribute reached which column, which is precisely what a
human staring at a terminal cannot check.

<<viewer test functions>>=
class FakeScreen:
    """A curses window that records what was drawn.

    Each ``addstr`` becomes a ``(row, column, text,
    attribute)`` tuple in ``calls``.  With ``fail_row`` set,
    writes to that row raise ``curses.error`` the way a real
    window does at the edges.
    """

    def __init__(self, height=10, width=80, fail_row=None):
        self.height = height
        self.width = width
        self.fail_row = fail_row
        self.calls = []

    def getmaxyx(self):
        return (self.height, self.width)

    def erase(self):
        self.calls.clear()

    def refresh(self):
        pass

    def addstr(self, row, column, text, attribute=0):
        import curses
        if row == self.fail_row:
            raise curses.error("out of bounds")
        self.calls.append((row, column, text, attribute))
@

The viewer under test is one finished run: it has a diff, output on
stdout, and a tag map to consult, so a draw of it exercises every
chunk above.  We give it a style table of distinct numbers rather than
real curses attributes, so an assertion names the kind that was meant
instead of a bit pattern that several kinds share.

<<viewer test functions>>=
def _drawable_viewer(workdir, **options):
    """A viewer over one finished run, with numbered styles."""
    import datetime
    from learnlog.commits import get_commits
    from learnlog.gitrepo import LearnlogRepo
    from learnlog.render import STYLES
    from learnlog.viewer import PlaybackViewer
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    (workdir / "hello.py").write_text('print("hi")\n')
    repo.begin_run(
        "hello.py", [],
        datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0),
    )
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="stdout: hello\n",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 1),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    commits = get_commits(repo)
    viewer = PlaybackViewer(
        repo, commits, len(commits) - 1, **options,
    )
    viewer.styles = {
        kind: number
        for number, kind in enumerate(STYLES, start=1)
    }
    return viewer
@

The first thing to check is that a kind survives the trip from
[[render]] to the screen at all---the card's headline and its section
heading are different kinds, and each must arrive with its own
attribute:

<<viewer test functions>>=
def test_draw_styles_each_line_by_its_kind(workdir):
    """A line reaches the screen with its kind's attribute."""
    pytest.importorskip("curses")
    from learnlog.render import KIND_SECTION, KIND_TITLE
    viewer = _drawable_viewer(workdir)
    screen = FakeScreen(height=24)

    viewer._draw(screen)

    drawn = {text: attribute for _, _, text, attribute in screen.calls}
    assert drawn["You ran hello.py"] == viewer.styles[KIND_TITLE]
    assert drawn["Terminal session"] == viewer.styles[KIND_SECTION]
@

A terminal-session line is the only line with two segments, and it is
the reason [[_segments]] exists.  The prefix must land at column~0 in
the faded style and the program's own text right after it in the
stream's style---if either half took the other's attribute the line
would read as learnlog's rather than the program's:

<<viewer test functions>>=
def test_draw_writes_an_io_line_as_prefix_then_text(workdir):
    """The faded prefix and the program's text are separate."""
    pytest.importorskip("curses")
    from learnlog.render import KIND_FADED, KIND_OUT
    viewer = _drawable_viewer(workdir)
    screen = FakeScreen(height=24)

    viewer._draw(screen)

    prefixes = [c for c in screen.calls if c[2] == "  stdout: "]
    assert len(prefixes) == 1
    row, column, _, attribute = prefixes[0]
    assert (column, attribute) == (0, viewer.styles[KIND_FADED])
    assert [c for c in screen.calls if c[0] == row] == [
        (row, 0, "  stdout: ", viewer.styles[KIND_FADED]),
        (
            row, len("  stdout: "), "hello",
            viewer.styles[KIND_OUT],
        ),
    ]
@

The clipping rule is what keeps that pair inside the window, and it is
cheaper to state against [[_segments]] than against a screen.  A
narrow window must cut the text short by the length of the prefix, not
by the width alone, and a window with no room must yield nothing
rather than an empty write:

<<viewer test functions>>=
def test_segments_clip_prefix_and_text_together():
    """Prefix plus text stop before the last column."""
    from learnlog.render import (
        KIND_FADED, KIND_OUT, StyledLine,
    )
    from learnlog.viewer import _segments
    line = StyledLine("hello there", KIND_OUT, "  stdout: ")

    assert _segments(line, 80) == [
        (0, "  stdout: ", KIND_FADED),
        (10, "hello there", KIND_OUT),
    ]

    narrow = _segments(line, 14)
    assert narrow == [
        (0, "  stdout: ", KIND_FADED),
        (10, "hel", KIND_OUT),
    ]
    assert sum(len(text) for _, text, _ in narrow) == 13

    assert _segments(line, 5) == [(0, "  st", KIND_FADED)]
    assert _segments(line, 1) == []
@

A window that refuses one row must not cost the reader the rest of the
screen.  This is the guard that has always been there; what is new is
that a test can see it work.  We compare against an undisturbed draw
rather than naming rows, since a card has blank lines and a blank line
is written nowhere:

<<viewer test functions>>=
def test_draw_survives_a_refused_row(workdir):
    """One failing row does not abort the draw."""
    pytest.importorskip("curses")
    viewer = _drawable_viewer(workdir)
    intact = FakeScreen(height=24)
    viewer._draw(intact)
    expected = {row for row, _, _, _ in intact.calls}
    assert 3 in expected

    screen = FakeScreen(height=24, fail_row=3)
    viewer._draw(screen)

    assert {
        row for row, _, _, _ in screen.calls
    } == expected - {3}
