\section{Batch playback}
\label{sec:play-batch}

The curses viewer requires a terminal, but sometimes we want to
pipe the output through [[less]] or redirect it to a file.
In batch mode the viewer simply prints all commits sequentially
to standard output.
This creates a natural reading flow: the captured output from
one commit appears just before the diff of the next, so the
reader sees what the program produced and what the student
changed as a result in a single continuous stream.

Batch mode has no navigation to trace, so every commit it emits gets
the same action word, [[batch]], and the order of the trace is the
order of the log.
The call sits after the [[detail is None]] test rather than at the top
of the loop, which is the batch spelling of the rule from
\cref{sec:viewer-view-trace}: a commit whose every run was filtered
away never reaches the screen, so it never reaches the trace either.
That an internal run stays out of the record of the review that
skipped it is the same consistency the reader sees---what the trailer
says was looked at is what standard output showed.

A tagged run announces its names on a [[Tags:]] line, and so does the
card the interactive viewer scrolls, because both get the line from
the same builder: a tag is a boundary every report reads
(\cref{sec:metrics-tags}), so the two places a student actually
looks at their history must show where the milestones sit.
The same now goes for the pause between runs.
Batch mode used to write both lines itself, ahead of the detail, while
the viewer prepended one of them in its own wording; they agreed only
because someone kept them agreeing.
Both are meta lines of the card now (\cref{sec:record-lines}), so the
two media cannot disagree about a tag or a gap---there is one place
that decides, and neither surface can reach it separately.

[[stream]] is resolved when the function is called, not when the
module is imported, because the test suite and the [[CliRunner]] both
work by replacing [[sys.stdout]] after import; a default argument
would capture the real one and write past them.
Colour is decided once per call rather than per line, since the
question [[use_color]] answers (\cref{sec:use-color}) is about the
destination and cannot change halfway down the log.

<<viewer functions>>=
def play_batch(repo, commits, start_index=0,
               filter_internal=True, trailers=None,
               tag_map=None, raw=False, color=None,
               stream=None):
    """Print commit records sequentially to stdout.

    When ``filter_internal`` is ``True``, learnlog's internal
    run blocks are stripped and commits that hold nothing else
    are skipped.  Review runs are printed like any other run.
    ``trailers`` is the map from ``get_run_trailers`` and
    ``tag_map`` the one from ``get_tag_map``; both are read
    from ``repo`` when not supplied.  With ``raw`` true the
    stored log format is printed instead of the reading view.
    ``color`` forces colour on or off; ``None`` writes it only
    to a terminal.  ``stream`` defaults to ``sys.stdout``.

    Every commit that is printed is reported to
    :func:`learnlog.record_view` with the action ``batch``.
    """
    if stream is None:
        stream = sys.stdout
    if trailers is None:
        trailers = get_run_trailers(repo)
    if tag_map is None:
        tag_map = get_tag_map(repo)
    colored = use_color(stream, color)
    for index in range(start_index, len(commits)):
        commit = commits[index]
        detail = get_commit_detail(
            repo, commit["hash"], filter_internal, trailers,
            reorder=False,
        )
        if detail is None:
            continue
        learnlog.record_view(commit["hash"], "batch")
        lines = record_lines(
            commit, detail,
            index=index,
            total=len(commits),
            elapsed=elapsed_since_previous_run(
                commits, index,
            ),
            tags=tag_map.get(commit["hash"], []),
            raw=raw,
        )
        for text in ansi_lines(lines, colored):
            stream.write(text)
        stream.write("\n")
@

